---
<QuestionLayout
  title="What is model_usage in Traverse, and why does my app fail with usage_undeclared?"
  description="Since v0.15.0, apps that pin models must declare model_usage: commercial or non_commercial. Without it, calls fail closed with usage_undeclared. Breaking change. Web value validation is traverse#1685."
  canonical="https://traverse-framework.com/questions/what-is-model-usage-in-traverse.html"
  category="Runtime and architecture"
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p><strong>Short answer:</strong> <code>model_usage</code> is how an app says it uses its pinned models: <code>commercial</code> or <code>non_commercial</code>. Since Traverse v0.15.0, an app with <code>exact_model_dependencies</code> must declare it. Without it, registration and execution fail closed with <code>usage_undeclared</code>. That is a breaking change (Spec 138 0.8.0, Decision 107). It is one more step in <strong>discover → execute → trace</strong>: the host checks the package's rights against your declared use before anything runs.</p>

  <h2>Where to set it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Rust</strong> — <code>ExactModelHostConnector::model_usage</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Web</strong> — <code>modelUsage</code> in the <code>ExactModelBrowserHost</code> options (<code>traverse-embedder-web@0.15.0</code>).</li>
    <li><strong>Swift</strong> — the <code>modelUsage:</code> argument of <code>ExactModelHost(pins:trustedPublicKeysHex:modelUsage:…)</code>. It has no default, so existing call sites stop compiling until you add it.</li>
    <li><strong>Kotlin and .NET</strong> — the <code>modelUsage</code> parameter of <code>ExactModelHost</code> in Maven Central <code>com.traverse-framework:traverse-embedder</code> 0.15.0 and nuget.org <code>TraverseEmbedder</code> 0.15.0. It is a nullable string, so pass the exact value.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What the host does with it</h2>
  <p>A <code>commercial</code> usage, declared by the app or forced by the host, rejects any package whose rights say <code>commercial_use: prohibited</code>, with <code>rights_policy_denied</code>. A <code>non_commercial</code> app can load it. Every result carries <code>model_evidence</code>, the verified rights record, so the trace shows which rights the call ran under. The rights themselves come from the signed package manifest (<code>license_id</code>, <code>attribution</code>, <code>redistribution</code>, <code>commercial_use</code>, <code>source_url</code>). An app cannot loosen them.</p>

  <h2>Known gaps in 0.15.0</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Web accepts any string.</strong> Published <code>traverse-embedder-web@0.15.0</code> does not check that the value is exactly <code>commercial</code> or <code>non_commercial</code>. A typo like <code>"Commercial"</code> skips the commercial check without an error. Tracked in <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/issues/1685">traverse#1685</a>. The fix (<a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/pull/1698">#1698</a>) merged on main on October 15 and rejects any other value with <code>usage_invalid</code>, but it is not in a release yet. Native Rust uses an enum and cannot express a bad value.</li>
    <li><strong>CLI verify has no usage flag.</strong> <code>traverse-cli model verify</code> in <code>traverse-cli-rs</code> 0.15.0 always verifies as <code>non_commercial</code>. <code>--model-usage</code> landed on main after the release (<a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/issues/1686">traverse#1686</a>) and is not in 0.15.0.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Until those ship, pass the exact value and test the commercial path once on purpose. No host is Certified under Spec 529. The upgrade notes are in <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/blob/v0.15.0/docs/upgrade-to-v0.15.0.md">docs/upgrade-to-v0.15.0.md</a>.</p>
</QuestionLayout>
